<h2>The background: conviction, appeal, and a rejected application</h2>
<p>The petitioner was convicted on 25 April 2018 by the Special Judge for POCSO Act cases, Jodhpur, in Sessions Case No. 116 of 2016 and sentenced to life imprisonment. In May 2026 the High Court partly allowed his criminal appeal, upholding the convictions under Sections 370(4), 376(2)(f), 506 and 354A of the IPC and Sections 7 and 8 of the POCSO Act. By the time of the present writ petition his custody, including remission, exceeded thirteen years, and his jail conduct was recorded as satisfactory.</p>
<p>His application for transfer to an open-air camp was rejected by the Open Air Camp Advisory Committee, Jaipur in September 2025, citing the conviction under Section 376(2)(f) IPC and the life sentence. The State also pointed to a separate 2023 conviction in Gujarat, against which an appeal remains pending, and invoked the eligibility restrictions in Rules 3(a), 3(d), 3(h) and 4(a) of the Rajasthan Prisoners Open Air Camp Rules, 1972.</p>

<h2>The Full Bench framework</h2>
<p>The decisive development came two weeks before this order: a Full Bench of the High Court, answering a reference on 26 October 2026, settled how sexual offence convictions interact with open-air camp eligibility. The Division Bench distilled and applied that framework:</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h3>The rejected approach</h3><p>Treat a conviction under Section 376 IPC or the POCSO Act as a categorical disqualification — the offence label alone decides the application.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h3>The mandated approach</h3><p>No absolute bar and no automatic right: the advisory committee must make an individualised, reasoned assessment of institutional conduct and reformative potential against the Rules&rsquo; criteria.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The logic tracks the constitutional grammar of sentencing administration. Open-air camps exist as instruments of reformation; a blanket exclusion by offence category substitutes the crime for the convict and leaves no room for the years of institutional record that follow the judgment. Equally, the gravity of sexual offences against children justifies searching scrutiny — which is precisely what an individualised assessment, as opposed to a rubber stamp in either direction, delivers.</p>

<h2>What the Court actually ordered</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>No transfer directed</strong> — the Court did not substitute its own view on eligibility</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Fresh decision ordered</strong> — the competent authority must decide the application in line with the Full Bench ruling and the earlier <em>Ajit Singh</em> precedent</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Three-week timeline</strong> — counted from the upload of the order, with consequential orders on any allowance to follow</div>
</div>
<p>The petition was disposed of on those terms. The order is a study in the limited but real role of judicial review in prison administration: the writ court does not pick the outcome, but it insists that the deciding authority ask the legally correct question.</p>

<h2>Where this sits in the post-conviction timeline</h2>
<p>It helps to place camp transfer among the distinct liberty-adjacent remedies available after conviction, because they are frequently confused. Suspension of sentence pending appeal addresses the interregnum before the appellate verdict; remission shortens the sentence itself under the applicable rules; premature release ends custody for eligible life convicts after the qualifying period; and open-air camp transfer changes the <em>conditions</em> of custody without shortening it. Each has its own authority, its own criteria and its own review mechanism. The present order concerned only the last — which is why thirteen years of custody and satisfactory conduct were relevant to the assessment, but the convictions themselves remained untouched and fully operative.</p>

<h2>Why this matters beyond Rajasthan</h2>
<p>Most Indian states run some version of graded custody — open prisons, semi-open institutions, or camps — and most impose offence-based screens on entry. The analytical move in this line of decisions, from categorical exclusion to individualised assessment, speaks to all of them. For POCSO convicts specifically, three practical consequences follow:</p>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>The institutional record becomes the case.</strong> Years of documented conduct, work, education and programme participation inside the prison are the raw material of an individualised assessment; convicts and their counsel should build and preserve that record deliberately.</li>
  <li><strong>Rejections must be reasoned.</strong> An order that recites the offence and the sentence, and nothing else, is now vulnerable; the committee must engage with conduct and reformative potential.</li>
  <li><strong>Other proceedings still count.</strong> A pending appeal elsewhere, or a second conviction, remains relevant material for the committee — the individualised approach cuts both ways.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Post-conviction practice is its own discipline. Counsel advising a convict&rsquo;s family should diarise the eligibility milestones under the applicable prison rules — minimum custody served, conduct certifications, committee meeting cycles — and ensure every application is supported by the jail&rsquo;s own records rather than bare assertions. Where a rejection rests only on the offence category, this line of authority supplies the ground of challenge; where it rests on an individualised evaluation, the writ court will rarely interfere.</p>
</div>
